 1 big thing: AI gets better at hacking vital systems 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios It's only a matter of time before freely available AI models will make it easier to hack critical services like power grids and water systems, Axios' Sam Sabin reports from new research. 

 Steve Swick, former top security executive at American Electric Power, told reporters during a briefing: "It's a clock that is moving faster than we've ever seen." 💧 Researchers at cybersecurity firm Frenos tested 10 AI models' ability to plan attacks against industrial systems, such as software that monitors water treatment processes.

 The models were fed information about potential hacking targets, and were asked to piece together the steps needed to achieve specific objectives. Three out of the 10 models successfully constructed attack plans in about 90% of attempts.

 Frenos did not disclose the names of the AI models tested. 👾 Frenos co-founder and CTO Harry Thomas says that once attackers gain access to an industrial network, AI could help them quietly map out systems and identify weaknesses without the same time constraints facing human hackers.

 "AI doesn't have the problem with patience. It doesn't need to eat. It doesn't need to sleep, and it doesn't need to use the restroom." Go deeper ... Get Axios Future of Cybersecurity . 

 
 
